{
 "type": "FeatureCollection",
 "metadata": {
  "note": "The Connecticut Western Railroad (chartered 25 Jun 1868) ran Hartford - Winsted - Canaan - Salisbury - State Line - Millerton from 21 Dec 1871 until its 31 Mar 1881 foreclosure reorganization as the Hartford and Connecticut Western, whose features (and the later CNE&W, PR&NE, Central New England and New Haven eras) are in central_new_england_railway.geojson. One feature, 1871-1881. Research report: research/branches/connecticut_western_railroad.txt.",
  "sources": [
   "https://www.loc.gov/item/sn84027718/ -- Connecticut Western News (Salisbury/Canaan CT), Chronicling America; company timetable ads 1871-1881",
   "https://www.loc.gov/item/sn84020071/ -- Litchfield Enquirer, Chronicling America",
   "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_New_England_Railway -- WCNE",
   "https://www.branchline.uk/jfpdf/southernnewenglandrrs2.pdf -- Fowler, Southern New England Railroads (2), lines 132, 147",
   "https://nornow.org/?p=13105 -- Norfolk Now, 6 Jun 2022",
   "https://lakevillejournal.com/life-changes-northwest-corner-coming-east-west-railroad -- Lakeville Journal, 20 Jan 2012",
   "https://www.traillink.com/trail/railroad-ramble -- Railroad Ramble trail (freight life, not passenger)"
  ],
  "operator_split": "Rule 4: 31 Mar 1881 Connecticut Western -> Hartford and Connecticut Western (a new corporation out of the foreclosure), following the CNE layer's split convention: this feature ends 1881 and the H&CW features (#4F7A3A) start 1881; both companies ran trains that year. The 27 Apr 1880 receivership is NOT a split (same company's receivers). New colour #AE137B, used by no other company in data/ or in the CNE layer. Maintainer alternative recorded by the report: if a foreclosure reorganization is judged the same real-world company, this feature should take #4F7A3A instead.",
  "omitted": [
   "Collinsville branch (Collinsville Junction - Collinsville village, ~0.5 mi, Dec 1874): Rule 5 - only CW-era passenger service proven is a horse car (CWN 29 Jan 1875); steam date unknown.",
   "Landon & Co. furnace branch at Chapinville: Rule 1, industrial freight siding.",
   "Pre-opening freight and ore trains Nov - 20 Dec 1871, and ore spurs around Salisbury / Ore Hill: Rule 1.",
   "1875 joint CW / R&C excursions and the announced Boston - Albany palace car over the Rhinebeck & Connecticut: R&C rails, covered by the CNE layer's rc-rhinecliff-state-line.",
   "Tariffville - Springfield branch: planned by the CW, built by the CNE in 1902; no CW-era service."
  ],
  "existing_layer_note": "No layer on dataset main or the CNE branch carries 'Connecticut Western' (report EXISTING LAYER CHECK; re-checked programmatically). The CNE layer starts at the H&CW in 1881.",
  "freight_only_audit": "Nothing drawn for freight-only years: the segment starts at the 21 Dec 1871 start of regular passenger trains and ends at the 1881 change of operator. Furnace branch and ore spurs not drawn.",
  "horsecar_audit": "Collinsville branch (horse car from Jan 1875) omitted in full. Main line steam from opening.",
  "line_label_audit": "Label follows Rule 6: 'Connecticut Western: Hartford – Millerton'.",
  "not_yet_sourced": [
   "Hartford terminal 1871-1881: Union Depot (shared with the Hartford & New Haven / HP&F) or a CW station at Spruce Street (~0.5 mi away; Fowler records a CNE Spruce Street station closed 1904)? Geometry drawn to Union Station.",
   "Collinsville branch traction: horse car in Jan 1875; when, or whether, steam trains replaced it is not established - branch left off.",
   "1874: no direct passenger evidence read (continuity only).",
   "1871 running time conflict (~10 mph per Norfolk Now vs ~16 mph per the garbled CWN timetable OCR); avg_speed_kmh and headway_min are estimates.",
   "1875 through palace car Boston - Hartford - CW - R&C - Albany: announced for 1 Jun 1875; whether it ran is not established.",
   "1877 'Millerton and Poughkeepsie' working: a CW car/train beyond Millerton, or a connection only?",
   "Rule 4 judgement on the 1881 reorganization (separate colour chosen; reuse of the H&CW colour is the alternative)."
  ]
 },
 "features": [
  {
   "type": "Feature",
   "properties": {
    "company": "Connecticut Western Railroad",
    "line": "Connecticut Western: Hartford – Millerton",
    "id": "cw-hartford-millerton",
    "color": "#AE137B",
    "mode": "intercity_rail",
    "avg_speed_kmh": 26,
    "speed_source": "ESTIMATE, unsourced as a figure. The 1871 running times CONFLICT: Norfolk Now (2022) says the first train left Hartford 8:40 a.m. and was due at Millerton 3:30 p.m. (~6 h 50 min for 68 mi, ~10 mph / ~16 km/h); the garbled OCR of the Connecticut Western News timetable of 22 Dec 1871 appears to put Millerton about 12.55 (~4 h 15 min, ~16 mph / ~26 km/h). 26 km/h takes the timetable reading; neither is confirmed. The Sep 1875 excursion (Hartford 7.00 - State Line 9.56, a special) implies ~23 mph and is not a regular train.",
    "geometry_source": "Reused from data/central_new_england_railway.geojson on the unmerged dataset branch (PR #72): hcw-hartford-state-line + hcw-state-line-millerton, concatenated. Those were routed with opstrace.py from OpenStreetMap via Overpass (maps.mail.ru mirror, Oct 2026; 'Griffins Industrial Track', 'Housatonic Railroad') and Wikimedia Commons map data (CC BY 4.0): Data:Central New England Railway.map ('Connecticut Western Railroad' line) and Data:Dutchess and Columbia Railroad.map.",
    "geometry_quality": "Traced 67.9 mi against 68 mi documented Hartford - Millerton (67.7 mi Hartford - State Line, Fowler line 147, plus ~1.0 mi State Line - Millerton; the company's Dec 1871 station list gives Millerton 68). Reused programmatically from the CNE layer (dataset PR #72) features hcw-hartford-state-line (66.9 mi vs 67.7) and hcw-state-line-millerton (1.0 mi vs 1.0), joined at their shared State Line vertex. Routing of the source traces: Commons:Connecticut Western Railroad 57.7 mi, OSM:Griffins Industrial Track 8.5 mi (Hartford - North Bloomfield, the surviving freight track), OSM:Housatonic Railroad 0.6 mi (Canaan), Commons:Dutchess and Columbia Railroad 1.0 mi (State Line - Millerton). No straight-line leg. West and north of North Bloomfield nothing of the grade is in OSM or OpenHistoricalMap, so ~58 mi is the Wikimedia Commons digitized right-of-way (Data:Central New England Railway.map, CC BY 4.0) plus ~1 mi from Data:Dutchess and Columbia Railroad.map - a digitization, not a survey, and not cross-checkable against OSM; it should hold the CW alignment through the Collinsville gorge - Pine Meadow section where the CW and the Canal Line cross twice, but that was not independently checked. Hartford end: drawn to the Union Station area. Which Hartford station CW trains used in 1871-1881 is unresolved (Union Depot vs the later CNE Spruce Street station, ~0.5 mi apart, Fowler line 147); if Spruce Street, the last ~0.5 mi is a later approach than this era used. No other post-1881 alignment change is known.",
    "segments": [
     {
      "start": 1871,
      "end": 1881,
      "headway_min": 240,
      "note": "Hartford - Blue Hills - Bloomfield - Tariffville - Hoskins - Simsbury - Stratton Brook - Canton - Collinsville - Pine Meadow - New Hartford - Winsted - Norfolk - East Canaan - Canaan - Salisbury - Lakeville - Ore Hill - State Line - Millerton (68 mi, the company's own Dec 1871 station list). Regular passenger trains began Thursday 21 Dec 1871 (Connecticut Western News 22 Dec 1871, printed timetable 'Commencing Thursday, Dec. 21st, 1871'): two through Hartford - Millerton trains each way plus a Millerton - Winsted mixed train each way; the first left Hartford 8.40 a.m. Freight and ore trains ran from Nov 1871 but carried no regular passengers before 21 Dec. 1877-1881 timetables show three or four westbound and four eastbound trains a day at Canaan, a Monday-only early train, a milk train, and short Winsted - Hartford workings. Connections: HP&F and Hartford & New Haven at Hartford; the Canal Line at Simsbury; the Naugatuck at Winsted; the Housatonic at Canaan (Union Depot); the Harlem, Dutchess & Columbia and (from 1875) Rhinebeck & Connecticut at Millerton / State Line. The State Line - Millerton mile was leased from the Dutchess & Columbia (Nov 1871) and every CW train ran over it from the first timetable, so it is folded into this feature; the D&C also ran its own trains on that mile. The road went into receivership on 27 Apr 1880 - the company's own receivers, the same company, so no split (the 13 Jun 1880 timetable is still the 'Conn. Western Railroad'). Ends with the 31 Mar 1881 foreclosure reorganization into the Hartford and Connecticut Western, a new corporation whose feature (CNE layer) starts 1881; both companies ran trains in 1881. Positive passenger evidence for every year except 1874, which rests on continuity (the line ran either side of it and the Collinsville agent had been in post 'nearly three years' by Jan 1875). The Collinsville branch (Dec 1874) is omitted under Rule 5: its only proven CW-era passenger service was a horse car (CWN 29 Jan 1875). Headway is an estimate from the 1871 and 1877-1881 train counts, not a timetable figure.",
      "sources": [
       "Segment-level: start (21 Dec 1871), service pattern and positive passenger years from the Connecticut Western News (Chronicling America sn84027718) issues of 1871-11-24, 1871-12-15, 1871-12-22, 1873-08-29, 1875-01-29, 1875-03-12, 1877-09-14, 1878-04-25, 1879-07-02, 1879-09-10, 1880-08-30 and 1881-10-26; Litchfield Enquirer 1872-08-29 (Housatonic ad) and 1880-02-26 (foreclosure); Wikipedia 'Central New England Railway' (charter, completion, 27 Apr 1880 bankruptcy, 31 Mar 1881 reorganization); Norfolk Now 6 Jun 2022 (first train); Lakeville Journal 20 Jan 2012 (1872 excursion); Fowler line 147 (1876 Pathfinder letters, mileage) - as read in research/branches/connecticut_western_railroad.txt, blocks [1]-[2]. 1874 by continuity only. Headway not sourced.",
       "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84027718/1871-11-24/ed-1/",
